<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luigi Cadorna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1850">1850</a>–<a href="/years/1928">1928</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Luigi Cadorna was chief of staff of the Italian army from 1914 to 1917. He launched eleven costly offensives on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soca">Isonzo</a> front and imposed harsh discipline, including summary executions. After the defeat at Caporetto in October–November 1917 he blamed his soldiers and was replaced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armando-diaz">Armando Diaz</a>; he was nevertheless made a marshal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1924.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luigi-cadorna.jpg" alt="Luigi Cadorna" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luigi_Cadorna_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Luigi Cadorna was born in 1850 at Pallanza in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, the son of General Raffaele Cadorna. He entered the Turin Military Academy at fifteen and was commissioned as a second lieutenant of artillery in 1868. In 1870 he took part in the occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in a force commanded by his father. As colonel of the 10th Regiment of Bersaglieri from 1892 he gained a reputation for strict discipline and harsh punishment, and he wrote a manual of infantry tactics that stressed the doctrine of the offensive. He became a lieutenant general in 1898.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Cadorna was first offered the post of chief of staff in 1908 and refused it over the question of political control in wartime. He accepted it in July 1914. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> entered the war on 24 May 1915 he commanded thirty-six infantry divisions of 875,000 men but only 120 modern artillery pieces. He chose to attack on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soca">Isonzo</a>, since the rest of the front against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> was judged even less suited to offence. The first battle there ended in stalemate, and ten more offensives followed. The Sixth Battle of the Isonzo, in August 1916, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorizia">Gorizia</a>; the Eleventh, in August 1917, broke through on the Bainsizza plateau.</p><p>On 24 October 1917 an Austro-Hungarian and German army attacked at Kobarid, known in Italian as Caporetto, and by 12 November had reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piave">Piave</a>. Cadorna had placed most of his troops far forward with little defence in depth, and he had been on leave for much of October. About 275,000 Italian soldiers were captured. The king and government dismissed him on 7 November, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armando-diaz">Armando Diaz</a> took his place. Cadorna was sent as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s representative to the Allied Supreme War Council at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>An inquiry into Caporetto, published in 1919, was highly critical of Cadorna. He answered with a two-volume memoir, La guerra alla fronte italiana. His reputation improved afterwards, and he was made a marshal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1924. He died at Bordighera in 1928.</p><p>Historians remain divided. During the war he dismissed 217 officers, and about 750 Italian soldiers were executed, the highest number in any army of the war. Claims that he revived decimation alienated his troops; John Keegan describes his severity instead as the summary execution of individual stragglers. Giorgio Rochat, while criticising his little concern for his soldiers, argues that trench warfare had a terrible logic of its own, independent of Cadorna and other commanders.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Luigi Cadorna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Pallanza, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Bordighera, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.92,8.55&amp;year=1900" aria-label="Pallanza on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4752 546 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Luigi Cadorna"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5202 816h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5202 816h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.92,8.55&amp;year=1900">What was here in 1900? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
